        <div class="tool-faq-accordion">
          <details class="tool-faq-item">
            <summary>
              <span>Why can pure black text on pure white backgrounds induce eye strain?</span>
              <svg class="faq-chevron" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor">
                <path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M5.293 7.293a1 1 0 011.414 0L10 10.586l3.293-3.293a1 1 0 111.414 1.414l-4 4a1 1 0 01-1.414 0l-4-4a1 1 0 010-1.414z" clip-rule="evenodd" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              At maximum 21:1 contrast, pure black (#000000) text against bright white (#FFFFFF) OLED or backlit displays can cause visual halation—a phenomenon where bright photons spill across the boundary of dark glyphs. This creates visual vibrations or blur, especially for readers with astigmatism or dyslexia. Softer pairings like deep slate (#0F172A) on warm off-white (#F8FAFC) maintain pristine AAA compliance (18.25:1) without optical fatigue.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="tool-faq-item">
            <summary>
              <span>Are disabled buttons exempt from the 4.5:1 contrast requirement?</span>
              <svg class="faq-chevron" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor">
                <path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M5.293 7.293a1 1 0 011.414 0L10 10.586l3.293-3.293a1 1 0 111.414 1.414l-4 4a1 1 0 01-1.414 0l-4-4a1 1 0 010-1.414z" clip-rule="evenodd" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              Yes. WCAG 2.1 Criterion 1.4.3 explicitly exempts text and UI components that are purely decorative or part of an inactive user interface element. However, modern inclusive design encourages avoiding disabled states where possible: keeping buttons active and displaying an informative inline error tooltip when clicked provides superior clarity for screen reader users.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="tool-faq-item">
            <summary>
              <span>How does font weight influence the required contrast ratio?</span>
              <svg class="faq-chevron" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor">
                <path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M5.293 7.293a1 1 0 011.414 0L10 10.586l3.293-3.293a1 1 0 111.414 1.414l-4 4a1 1 0 01-1.414 0l-4-4a1 1 0 010-1.414z" clip-rule="evenodd" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              Under WCAG 2.1, typography that is bold (font weight ≥700) qualifies as "Large Text" at 14pt (~18.5px), lowering the required threshold from 4.5:1 down to 3.0:1. Conversely, ultra-thin weights (such as 100 or 200) pass the mathematical 4.5:1 threshold in software but remain illegible in practice due to thin stroke width, which the emerging APCA standard actively addresses.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="tool-faq-item">
            <summary>
              <span>Does this tool transmit inputted hex codes or brand palettes to external servers?</span>
              <svg class="faq-chevron" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor">
                <path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M5.293 7.293a1 1 0 011.414 0L10 10.586l3.293-3.293a1 1 0 111.414 1.414l-4 4a1 1 0 01-1.414 0l-4-4a1 1 0 010-1.414z" clip-rule="evenodd" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              Zero network transmissions occur. All RGB conversions, sRGB gamma de-linearizations, and relative luminance calculations execute entirely in your local browser runtime via client-side JavaScript. Proprietary brand color palettes and unreleased design tokens remain completely secure on your machine.
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
